# `s-maxage` bounds the EDGE separately from the browser, and that split is
# the whole point. These filenames are content-hashed, so a browser can hold
# one forever and `max-age=31536000, immutable` says so. Cloudflare's edge is
# the part that cannot be trusted to hold one forever: a request that lands
# while a deploy is still propagating gets index.html back with a 200 (the SPA
# catch-all in _redirects answers anything Pages cannot serve as a file), and
# whatever the edge caches at a content-hashed URL is never revalidated,
# because the filename never changes. Observed live, twice: once as an
# unstyled site (a stylesheet served as text/html) and once on two favicons
# from a single deploy.
#
# 24 hours turns "poisoned until a human notices and purges" into "poisoned
# until tomorrow at worst", at no cost worth measuring -- the edge simply
# re-fetches from Pages, which is free, fast, and same-datacentre. Browsers
# still never re-ask.
#
# A URL purge is NOT a reliable fix for this, checked directly: purging the
# exact URL left the poisoned response in place three times running, and only
# `purge_everything` cleared it. The bad entry is keyed on the `Origin` header
# a browser sends and curl does not, so a single-file purge clears a different
# variant than the one being served.
# Security headers for every response. Deliberately declares no
# Cache-Control -- caching is per-asset-type and lives in the blocks below.
# Cloudflare Pages applies EVERY matching rule and merges them, so this block
# and the more specific ones underneath both take effect rather than one
# winning outright.
#
# No Content-Security-Policy here yet, on purpose, and its absence is the one
# thing in this file worth reading before adding it. A single site-wide
# `script-src 'self'` would break iPhone photo uploads: src/admin/heic.ts
# dynamically imports heic-to, whose decoder runs inside a Worker built from
# a blob URL, and a blob worker inherits the creating document's CSP rather
# than carrying one of its own. That decoder's emscripten glue builds its
# method callers through the dynamic-code constructor at runtime -- a live
# path, not a dead branch, checked in the shipped dist/assets/heic-to-*.js --
# so the only policy that lets it run is 'unsafe-eval' on whatever document
# created the worker. Granting that site-wide, to every visitor, to support
# one admin-only conversion is a worse trade than shipping the headers below
# now and splitting the policy by path afterwards.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options is not decoration on this site specifically: it has
# twice served a response under the wrong Content-Type through a poisoned
# edge cache (see the comment above), and sniffing is the step that turns a
# wrong Content-Type into executed script.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security carries no `preload` token, deliberately. Preload
# is a submission into a list compiled into browsers, it is slow and awkward
# to undo, and this is still running on a throwaway test host. `max-age` and
# `includeSubDomains` are reversible; preload effectively is not.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), magnetometer=(), gyroscope=(), accelerometer=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable, s-maxage=86400

/*.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/*.pdf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, must-revalidate

/build-info.json
  Cache-Control: no-store
